<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rafael Urdaneta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1788">1788</a>–<a href="/years/1845">1845</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rafael Urdaneta was a Venezuelan general of the wars of independence and a close ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>. He served as minister of war of Gran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> and in September 1830 seized power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a>, hoping to recall Bolívar. After Bolívar's death his regime collapsed in 1831. He later held office in Venezuela and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rafael-urdaneta.jpg" alt="Rafael Urdaneta" width="320" height="355" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martín Tovar y Tovar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rafael_urdaneta.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rafael José Urdaneta y Farías was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maracaibo">Maracaibo</a> on 24 October 1788 to a prominent family of Spanish descent. He began his schooling in Maracaibo and continued in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a>, studying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and philosophy. In 1804 he went to Santa Fe, encouraged by his uncle Martín de Urdaneta y Troconis, an official of the Real Audiencia, and spent several years there gaining experience in military administration. On 31 August 1822 he married Dolores Vargas París, a young heroine of Santa Fe.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Urdaneta joined the junta formed in Santa Fe on 20 July 1810 and entered the patriot army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a> as a lieutenant on 1 November 1810. Captured by royalists after the Battle of Santa Fe in 1813, he was held for a few months. He then joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>'s Admirable Campaign, distinguishing himself at the Battle of Niquitao on 2 July 1813 and at the Battle of Taguanes. He later led actions including the Battle of Barbula, the Caracas Exodus of 1814, the Siege of Santa Fe, after which he became a lieutenant-general at 26, the Capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maracaibo">Maracaibo</a> and the march to San Carlos in 1821. Bolívar called him El Brillante for his strategy.</p><p>As Minister of War in 1828 Urdaneta presided over the cabinet that tried the September Conspiracy, an attempt on Bolívar's life. Convinced that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-paula-santander">Francisco de Paula Santander</a> led it, he and most ministers sentenced him to death, but Bolívar, fearing for the union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a> and Venezuela, imposed lifelong exile instead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In 1830 enmity between New Granadians and Venezuelans reached a head in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a>, where the Venezuelan battalion <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a>, loyal to Bolívar, defeated a battalion loyal to Santander. President Joaquín Mosquera and Vice President Domingo Caycedo fled, and on 5 September 1830 Urdaneta took power as Provisional Chief of the Government. He and his allies hoped to persuade Bolívar, who had resigned in May, to return. When Bolívar would not, Urdaneta called Congress to meet at Villa de Leyva on 15 June 1831.</p><p>Before it met, generals José María Obando and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-hilario-lopez">José Hilario López</a> took the southern provinces and José Salvador Córdova Muñoz the northern ones. On 14 April 1831 they proclaimed Caycedo head of the executive. Urdaneta negotiated, and the Treaty of Apulo of 28 April 1831 ended the fighting and ended his rule. He and his wife fled, were persecuted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maracaibo">Maracaibo</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-antonio-paez">José Antonio Páez</a> and went to Curaçao. Back in Venezuela from 1832, he sat as a senator until 1845. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 23 August 1845 on his way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> as Venezuela's envoy and is buried in the National Pantheon of Venezuela.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rafael Urdaneta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>General <b>Rafael Urdaneta</b> seized power in Bogotá in September, hoping to recall Bolívar, but Bolívar died near Santa Marta on 17 December 1830.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maracaibo">Maracaibo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/venezuela">Venezuela</a>. Died in Venezuela. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=10.63,-71.63&amp;year=1825" aria-label="Maracaibo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2577 1767 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Rafael Urdaneta"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3027 2037h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3027 2037h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=10.63,-71.63&amp;year=1825">What was here in 1825? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
